2.1.3 Questions
1. Identify and indicate each component of the FOR loop in the program code of Experiment 1.

Answer: In for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++):
- int i = 1 is the initialization. It creates the counter and sets its starting value.
- i <= 10 is the loop condition. It is checked before every iteration, and the loop stops when it becomes false.
- i++ is the update. It increases the counter by 1 after every iteration.
- The statements inside { ... } are the loop body, which runs once for each of the 10 students.
2. Why is highest initialized to 0 and lowest initialized to 100? What happens if highest is initialized to 100 and lowest is initialized to 0?
Answer: Scores are assumed to be between 0 and 100. Starting highest at 0 means any valid score is greater than or equal to it, so it can replace it. 
Starting lowest at 100 means any valid score is less than or equal to it, so it can replace it. 
If highest starts at 100 and lowest starts at 0, then score > highest and score < lowest can never be true for scores in the range 0-100. 
The program would always print a highest score of 100 and a lowest score of 0, which is wrong.

3. Explain the function and workflow of the following code snippet.
java
   if (score > highest) {
       highest = score;
   }
   if (score < lowest) {
       lowest = score;
   }
- Answer: The first if compares the current score with highest. If the current score is larger, it becomes the new highest. 
The second if compares the current score with lowest. If the current score is smaller, it becomes the new lowest. 
Both checks are executed on every iteration, so after the 10th student highest and lowest hold the maximum and minimum of all scores entered.

4. Modify the program to count how many students pass and fail, based on the passing threshold (minimum score of 60).
Answer: Two counters, passCount and failCount, are declared and set to 0 before the loop. 
Inside the loop, if (score >= 60) increases passCount; otherwise failCount is increased. After the loop, both totals are displayed. 
With the test data above, 9 students pass and 1 student fails.

2.2 Experiment 2: Student Grades Case Study in SIAKAD — WHILE Loop

The program asks how many students' scores will be entered. It validates each score. Scores below 0 or above 100 are rejected and must be entered again.
Valid scores are converted to letter grades according to the following ranges: 
A (>80-100), B+ (>73-80), B (>65-73), C+ (>60-65), C (>50-60), D (>39-50), and E (0-39).

import java.util.Scanner;
public class SiakadWhile264107020237 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int studentCount;
        int i = 0;
        int score;

        System.out.print("Enter the number of students:");
        studentCount = sc.nextInt();

        while (i < studentCount) {
            System.out.print("Enter score for student " +(i + 1) +  ": ");
            score = sc.nextInt();

            if (score < 0 || score > 100) {
                System.out.println("Invalid score, Please enter a valid score! ");
                continue;
            }

            if (score > 80 && score <= 100) {
                System.out.println("The grade of student " + (i + 1) + " is A");
            } else if (score > 73 && score <= 80) {
                System.out.println("The grade of student " + (i + 1) + " is B+");
            } else if (score > 65 && score <= 73) {
                System.out.println("The grade of student " + (i + 1) + " is B");
            } else if (score > 60 && score <= 65) {
                System.out.println("The grade of student " + (i + 1) + " is C+");
            }  else if (score > 50 && score <= 60) {
                System.out.println("The grade of student " + (i + 1) + " is C");
            } else if (score > 39 && score <= 50) {
               System.out.println("The grade of student " + (i + 1) + " is D");
            }  else {
                System.out.println("The grade of student " + (i + 1) + " is E");
                }
                i++;
            }
        }   
    }

        
