2.2.3 Questions

1.Explain the code score < 0 || score > 100 and the continue statement.

Answer: score < 0 || score > 100 uses the logical OR operator. It is true when the score is below 0 or above 100, which means the score is outside the valid range.
The continue statement skips the rest of the current iteration and jumps back to the loop condition. 
In this program, an invalid score is not graded, and because i++ is skipped, the same student is asked to enter a score again.

2. Why is i++ written at the end of the WHILE loop? What happens if it is written at the beginning?

Answer: i++ is written at the end so that the counter increases only after a valid score has been graded. When an invalid score is entered, 
continue skips i++, so the program asks for the same student again. If i++ were written at the beginning of the loop, 
the counter would increase even for invalid scores. The invalid entry would then use up one student's slot, 
the student numbers shown in the messages would be wrong, and fewer than the requested number of students would be graded.
3. If the number of students entered is 19, how many times will the WHILE loop run?
Answer: The loop produces exactly 19 valid grades. If every score entered is valid, the loop runs exactly 19 times. 
If some scores are invalid, each invalid entry causes one extra iteration, so the loop runs 19 + (number of invalid entries) times.

