2.3.3 Questions

1. If the first customer name entered is cancel, how many times is the loop executed?

Answer: The loop body is executed once. A do-while loop runs its body first and checks the condition afterwards,
so the customer name is always requested at least one time. Because the name is cancel, the program prints 
"Transaction canceled." and break exits the loop immediately.

2. State the stopping condition used in this DO-WHILE loop.
Answer: The loop stops when the cashier enters cancel as the customer name.
The comparison uses equalsIgnoreCase, so cancel, Cancel, and CANCEL all work.
The break statement then exits the loop.

3. What is the function of using the value true in the DO-WHILE condition?
Answer: while (true) makes the loop condition always true,
so the loop repeats indefinitely. It lets the cashier serve any number of customers, 
and the only way out is the break statement that runs when cancel is entered.

4. Why does this DO-WHILE loop still run even though it has no initialization and update components?
Answer: Unlike a for loop, a do-while loop does not need a counter. It only needs a condition. 
Here, the condition is the constant true, and the exit is controlled by the user's input through break.
The body supplies the data itself (the customer name is read inside the loop), so no separate initialization or update is required.

